</section>

<section class="moves" id="moves" aria-labelledby="moves-title">
  <h2 id="moves-title">Three moves</h2>
  <ol class="move-list">
    <li class="move">
      <span class="move-num" aria-hidden="true">01</span>
      <div class="move-body">
        <h3>Clean</h3>
        <p>Remove dependency folders, build output and caches. You see every path with its size
          before anything happens, and only paths classified as regenerable are ever touched.</p>
        <p class="move-safety">A folder it cannot classify stays on disk. Guessing is not a mode.</p>
      </div>
    </li>
    <li class="move">
      <span class="move-num" aria-hidden="true">02</span>
      <div class="move-body">
        <h3>Archive</h3>
        <p>Clean, compress everything that remains into a local store, and remove the working copy.
          Days or gigabytes later, the project is one small, verified archive.</p>
        <p class="move-safety">Uncommitted work raises a warning with modified and untracked counts
          before anything is packed.</p>
      </div>
    </li>
    <li class="move move-wake">
      <span class="move-num" aria-hidden="true">03</span>
      <div class="move-body">
        <h3>Restore</h3>
        <p>Unpack to the original path, verify every file against the manifest checksums, and show
          the exact install commands before running a single one. Your tools are found where you
          installed them — npm under nvm, pnpm under Homebrew, uv, cargo, go — and a missing tool
          fails loudly instead of being skipped.</p>
        <p class="move-safety">If verification fails, the archive stays put and the extracted tree is
          left for inspection.</p>
      </div>
    </li>
  </ol>

  <div class="restore-proof">
    <p class="restore-label">What Restore shows before it runs anything</p>
    <pre><code>Restore  ~/Projects/heatmap
  npm install        package-lock.json
  cargo build        Cargo.toml

Nothing runs until you confirm.</code></pre>
  </div>

  <p class="also">From the app: <strong>Project Info</strong> (local size versus core versus
    regenerable, git status, branch, remote), <strong>Open</strong> in VS Code, and
    <strong>Open Repository</strong> in the browser. From Finder, a <strong>Dormant ▸</strong>
    right-click menu: Clean, Archive, Restore and Import Folder in Dormant on a folder; Open
    Directory in Dormant on empty space — and <strong>Git Clone into Folder in Dormant</strong>:
    paste a repository URL and the clone lands in that folder, registered and open in VS Code.</p>
  <p class="also">In the main window: <strong>search</strong> across projects and paths,
    <strong>Stale</strong> badges for anything without a commit in 30 days, a live savings summary
    with one-click <strong>Clean All…</strong> (previewed per project first), and a quiet note when
    a project sits idle past 30 days — Dormant suggests archiving and never does it on its own.
    The menu bar lists your projects for one-click <strong>Open</strong> and
    <strong>Restore</strong>.</p>
</section>

